## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical lazy-loading with ES module dynamic import, closures, and promises in a dashboard scenario.

## Key Concepts
- dynamic import() and module caching
- per-widget state gated by closures
- IntersectionObserver for visibility-triggered loads
- cancellation with AbortController
- timeout handling and graceful fallbacks

## Code Example
```javascript
function createWidgetLoader(){
  const registry = new Map();
  function loadWidget(id){
    const entry = registry.get(id);
    if (entry && entry.state === 'ready') return Promise.resolve(entry.module);
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    const p = import(`./widgets/${id}.js`).then(m => {
      if (ctrl.signal.aborted) throw new Error('aborted');
      registry.set(id, { state: 'ready', module: m, promise: Promise.resolve(m) });
      return m;
    }).catch(e => { registry.set(id, { state: 'error' }); throw e; });
    registry.set(id, { state: 'loading', promise: p, controller: ctrl });
    return p;
  }
  return { loadWidget, isLoaded: id => registry.get(id)?.state === 'ready' };
}
```

